WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:19.010
<v Ira Glass>For three weeks, Joe Biden said as forcefully as a human being possibly could say that he would not step down. And for three weeks, it was this agonizing drip, drip, drip of people trying to get him to reconsider and of voters around the country wondering if he was going to reconsider.
<v Ira Glass>在过去三周里，乔·拜登以人类所能表达的最强硬的态度声称他不会退选。而在那三周里，这是一个令人痛苦的缓慢过程：人们不断尝试让他重新考虑，全国各地的选民也在猜测他是否会回心转意。

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:36.030
<v Ira Glass>Fed by a steady stream of daily news stories that read tea leaves and applied electron microscopes to the tiniest scraps of evidence, looking for any minuscule signs indicating that maybe it could happen, maybe Biden would rethink things. Democrats wondering what it might take to change Biden's mind.
<v Ira Glass>每天不断的各种新闻报道，都在仔细揣摩其言外之意，甚至用“电子显微镜”去审视最微小的证据，试图寻找任何一点点他可能改变主意的迹象。民主党人则在猜测，到底需要什么才能改变拜登的想法。

00:00:36.030 --> 00:01:08.100
<v Ira Glass>Meanwhile, over in the other half of the country, in red-state America, at the Republican National Convention, people who had changed their minds and reconsidered firmly held convictions were a big part of the festivities. Donald Trump picked his vice president, a man who once said about him, "My god, what an idiot," JD Vance. Nikki Haley, who had once called Trump unhinged, and other former Trump rivals, people who once seemed like they hated Trump and spoken out against him, took the stage to show how they'd reconsidered.
<v Ira Glass>与此同时，在国家另一半的“红州”美国，共和党全国代表大会上，那些已经改变想法、重塑坚定信念的人成了庆典的重要组成部分。唐纳德·特朗普选择了他的副总统人选——万斯（JD Vance），一个曾经称他为“天哪，真是个白痴”的人。曾经称特朗普为“精神错乱”的尼基·黑利（Nikki Haley）以及其他曾经的对手，那些看似憎恨特朗普并公开发表反对言论的人，纷纷登台展示他们是如何改变看法的。

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:33.120
<v Ira Glass>And even Trump himself, the centerpiece of the whole thing, seemed to be presenting himself as a changed man, somebody who had reconsidered who he wants to be in the wake of his near assassination. He presented a notably gentler, uniter-not-divider side of himself. All this reconsideration, of course, was in the service of getting the swing voters of the swing states to do some reconsideration of their own about who they might be picking for president this fall.
<v Ira Glass>甚至特朗普本人，作为整个事件的核心，似乎也表现得像个改过自新的人，一个在险些遭遇暗杀后重新思考自己想要成为什么样的人。他表现出了明显更温和、团结者而非分裂者的一面。当然，所有这些“重新考虑”都是为了让摇摆州的摇摆选民对今年秋天该选谁当总统进行他们自己的重新考虑。

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:53.680
<v Ira Glass>I sometimes feel like the rarest thing in the world is to witness somebody actually changing their mind. You know what I mean? Changing their mind about something big, some fundamental belief. I mean, I know it happens, right? Most of us know people who are never going to have kids who decide they're going to go for it, or Democrats who became Republicans, pro-lifers who switched to pro-choicers.
<v Ira Glass>我有时觉得世界上最罕见的事情就是见证一个人真正改变自己的想法。你知道我的意思吗？改变对某件大事、某种基本信仰的看法。我是说，我知道这确实会发生，对吧？我们大多数人都认识那些原本不想要孩子却决定尝试的人，或者从民主党转为共和党的人，从反堕胎主义者转变为支持堕胎权利的人。

00:01:53.680 --> 00:02:05.210
<v Ira Glass>When it happens, it's a seismic thing. And that point of transition, when you're flipping from one way of seeing things to the new way, that is a weird, awkward thing to live through.
<v Ira Glass>当这种情况发生时，它是一场地震。而那个过渡点，当你从一种看待事物的方式翻转到新方式的时候，那是一种极其尴尬、奇特的经历。

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:36.550
<v Ira Glass>And so today on our show, in this moment when Joe Biden seems to have reconsidered some of his most deeply held ideas about himself and his chances in this election, we have stories of people rethinking some big, basic beliefs that they've taken for granted, trying on a new attitude toward the world. And we watch how that goes for them. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>所以今天在我们的节目中，在这个乔·拜登似乎已经重新考虑了他对自己及竞选胜算的一些最根深蒂固的想法的时刻，我们讲述了一些人重新思考他们理所当然的一些重大基本信念，尝试以新态度对待世界的故事。我们来看看他们的情况如何。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:02:36.550 --> 00:03:02.410
<v Ira Glass>Act one, Fate of the Union. Of all the people who'd rethought their feelings about Donald Trump at the Republican National Convention in Milwaukee this week, there was one guest for whom the rethink has been especially dramatic, and that's Sean O'Brien. A lifelong Democrat from Massachusetts, Sean O'Brien is the head of the Teamsters Union. 1.3 million truck drivers, rail, air, all transportation, delivery workers, lots of jobs, they endorsed Biden in 2020.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，联邦的命运。在本周密尔沃基共和党全国代表大会上，所有重新考虑对唐纳德·特朗普看法的人中，有一位嘉宾的转变尤为戏剧性，那就是肖恩·奥布莱恩（Sean O'Brien）。作为一名来自马萨诸塞州的终身民主党人，肖恩·奥布莱恩是卡车司机工会（Teamsters Union）的负责人。该工会有130万名卡车司机、铁路、航空、所有运输及配送工人，他们曾在2020年支持拜登。

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:26.170
<v Ira Glass>And Sean O'Brien was at the RNC to give a speech-- a big speech, actually, the keynote speech on the first night of the convention. I should say, the Teamsters Union president hasn't spoken at the RNC ever before in the entire history of the union, because-- maybe this is obvious-- Republicans have been the party of big business and generally pretty opposed to organized labor. Zoe Chace tells how this played out.
<v Ira Glass>肖恩·奥布莱恩在共和党全国代表大会上发表了演讲，实际上是第一晚的基调演讲。我得说，卡车司机工会主席在大会历史上从未在共和党全代会上发过言，因为——或许这显而易见——共和党一直是代表大企业的政党，通常非常反对有组织工会。佐伊·蔡斯（Zoe Chace）讲述了这一切是如何发生的。

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:55.590
<v Zoe Chace>Here's how weird it is that this guy, Sean O'Brien, is at the RNC in the first place. President Biden, a Democrat, calls himself the most pro-union president in history. There's reasons. He bailed out union pensions. The Teamsters were pushing for that. All his major infrastructure bills have included money and benefits specifically for unions. He walked a picket line with the United Auto Workers. He gave his first 2020 campaign speech from a Teamsters Hall.
<v Zoe Chace>这个叫肖恩·奥布莱恩的家伙出现在共和党全代会本身就非常诡异。拜登总统，一位民主党人，自称是有史以来最支持工会的总统。这是有原因的：他救助了工会的养老金，卡车司机工会曾为此推动；他所有的重大基础设施法案都包含了专门针对工会的资金和福利；他与全美汽车工人联合会一起参加了罢工；他在2020年的第一次竞选演讲也是在卡车司机大厅发表的。

00:03:55.590 --> 00:04:21.660
<v Zoe Chace>Donald Trump has a record, too, as a Republican president, of rolling back regulations that protected unions, appointing Supreme Court justices who ruled against unions, and of course, hiring non-union workers when he was a real estate developer, notoriously stiffing his workers out of overtime. Both of them talk about loving American workers, obviously. That's politics. But their policies are very different.
<v Zoe Chace>唐纳德·特朗普作为共和党总统也有过往记录：取消保护工会的法规，任命反对工会的最高法院法官，当然，还有他在担任房地产开发商时雇用非工会工人，且因臭名昭著地克扣工人的加班费而闻名。显然，他们两人都声称热爱美国工人，这就是政治。但他们的政策截然不同。

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:34.740
<v Zoe Chace>So when Teamsters President Sean O'Brien showed up at the Republican Convention this week, he felt like an outsider right away. Or maybe he wanted to make the point that he was an outsider right away.
<v Zoe Chace>所以，当卡车司机工会主席肖恩·奥布莱恩本周出现在共和党大会上时，他立刻感觉自己像个局外人。或者，也许他就是想强调自己是个局外人。

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:45.720
<v Sean O'Brien>So I'm backstage. And the rehearsal the night before, we came in, and they just wanted to show us the setup of the stage. So--
<v Sean O'Brien>所以我正在后台。前一天晚上的排练我们进场时，他们只是想向我们展示舞台的布局。所以——

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:51.520
<v Zoe Chace>They're telling Sean how to walk across the stage to the podium. He's going to be the keynote speaker on night one.
<v Zoe Chace>他们正在告诉肖恩如何穿过舞台走到讲台。他将是第一晚的基调演讲者。

00:04:51.520 --> 00:05:11.520
<v Sean O'Brien>And so they said, no, you got to do a dogleg right when you come out of the stage and come down. But the gentleman said, you know golf? You know how you do a dogleg right? I said, look, in my business, if you're a good golfer, you're a bad representative. I don't golf. So he says, just make, like, a little arc.
<v Sean O'Brien>他们说，你从舞台出来时必须先走个右狗腿弯（dogleg right）。但我问那位先生，你懂高尔夫吗？你知道什么是右狗腿弯吗？我说，听着，在我的行业里，如果你是个好的高尔夫球手，那你就是个糟糕的代表。我不打高尔夫。于是他说，那就画个小弧线。

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:13.140
<v Zoe Chace>He's like, it was a bad example. OK.
<v Zoe Chace>他说这是一个坏例子。好吧。

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:31.150
<v Sean O'Brien>So here I am, hopefully delivering the most powerful speech for American workers. And I'm debating what a dogleg is with the person. So it actually-- it was typical to my nature. I get distracted and start talking to him about how union officials shouldn't golf because they should be out working.
<v Sean O'Brien>所以，我希望能为美国工人发表最有力的演讲。而我却在跟那个人争论什么是狗腿弯。这实际上很符合我的天性。我会分心，然后开始跟他谈论工会官员不应该打高尔夫球，因为他们应该出去工作。

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:56.230
<v Zoe Chace>Sean O'Brien seems to be here to walk a fine line, basically, with Republicans. He's like, look, this is a major prime-time slot where you don't expect to hear from us, where we get to talk to people who aren't usually listening. He describes his mission as enlightening the Republican National Convention on how important workers and unions are to this country, and maybe make some new political allies.
<v Zoe Chace>肖恩·奥布莱恩似乎是为了在共和党人之间走钢丝。他说，看，这是一个黄金时段，你们通常不会听到我们的声音，在这里我们可以接触到平时不听我们说话的人。他描述自己的使命是向共和党大会阐明工人和工会对这个国家的重要性，并可能建立一些新的政治盟友。

00:05:56.230 --> 00:06:12.090
<v Zoe Chace>He's been on this charm offensive with Republicans for months. He went to Mar-a-Lago. He met with Trump, took a thumbs-up picture with him. He's been meeting with Republican senators, including hard-line conservative Josh Hawley, who ended up walking union picket lines.
<v Zoe Chace>几个月来，他一直对共和党人进行这种“魅力攻势”。他去了海湖庄园，会见了特朗普，并与他合影竖起大拇指。他还一直在会见共和党参议员，包括强硬派保守党人乔什·霍利（Josh Hawley），后者最终也加入了工会的罢工纠察线。

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:37.020
<v Zoe Chace>O'Brien told me, we have a really diverse union in all the ways, including politically. A big chunk of his membership supports Trump, and he's here partly for them. But some of organized labor is not happy with him at all. It's hard to overstate. Appalled, enraged, apoplectic, horrified. A vice president of the Teamsters Union publicly called it unconscionable.
<v Zoe Chace>奥布莱恩告诉我，我们的工会在各方面都非常多元化，包括政治上。他很大一部分会员支持特朗普，他来这里部分也是为了他们。但一些有组织工会的人对他非常不满。这很难被夸大。感到震惊、愤怒、暴跳如雷、恐怖。卡车司机工会的一位副主席公开称这“不可理喻”。

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:44.860
<v Rick Smith>So as I think of O'Brien going to the RNC and doing his song and dance, I got to go, what's the message? What's he going to say?
<v Rick Smith>所以当我想到奥布莱恩去共和党全代会唱歌跳舞时，我不得不问，信息是什么？他打算说什么？

00:06:44.860 --> 00:07:04.150
<v Zoe Chace>This is Rick Smith's radio show. He's a 35-year Teamster, a truck driver. He thinks O'Brien's just going to be part of the Trump campaign's long-running political theater of being a party for blue-collar workers without making any actual policy that helps those workers. To him, it's like, what's O'Brien going to get for this?
<v Zoe Chace>这是里克·史密斯（Rick Smith）的广播节目。他是一名有35年工龄的卡车司机。他认为奥布莱恩只是在参与特朗普竞选团队长期以来的政治表演，即假装自己是一个为蓝领工人服务的政党，却没有任何实际政策能真正帮助那些工人。对他来说，他在想：奥布莱恩能从中得到什么？

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:24.770
<v Rick Smith>The reality is is Sean O'Brien is going to be their whipping boy. They are going to use him and my Teamsters Union as their hood ornament as they drive their anti-union policy truck right over the top of working people. And the reality is is Sean O'Brien is going to be their dancing show pony, whether he wants to be or not.
<v Rick Smith>现实情况是，肖恩·奥布莱恩将成为他们的“替罪羊”。他们会利用他以及我的卡车司机工会作为“汽车引擎盖上的装饰品”，同时用他们的反工会政策之车碾压工人阶级。现实情况是，无论肖恩·奥布莱恩是否愿意，他都将成为他们表演的“舞动小马”。

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:51.180
<v Rick Smith>And they're going to ride him until election day, because what they understand, what Trump understands, what the RNC gets by giving him this platform, is a lot of his members vote for Republicans. This is making it OK. This is going, yeah, don't worry about your job. Don't worry about any of that stuff. It's OK. It's fine.
<v Rick Smith>他们会利用他直到选举日。因为他们明白，特朗普明白，共和党全代会给予他这个平台是因为很多他的会员投票给共和党。这让一切变得名正言顺。这就像在说：没事的，别担心你的工作，别担心那些东西。没事的，挺好。

00:07:51.180 --> 00:08:16.920
<v Zoe Chace>So to see how this song and dance goes over, I pick my spot way up high in the stands and sit with some delegates. I find Todd Gilman, this delegate from Michigan I know, and make him watch Sean O'Brien's speech with me. Todd's retired, a Navy vet. He was in a union decades ago. He got involved with the Republican Party just a few years ago. And he's organizing a county commissioner race now. We're chatting.
<v Zoe Chace>所以为了看看这场表演效果如何，我在看台高处找了个位置，和一些代表坐在一起。我找到了我认识的密歇根州代表托德·吉尔曼（Todd Gilman），让他跟我一起看肖恩·奥布莱恩的演讲。托德已经退休了，是名海军退伍军人。他几十年前加入过工会，几年前才开始参与共和党事务。他现在正在组织一场县专员竞选。我们正在聊天。

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.720
<v Zoe Chace>How's that going, those races or those campaigns?
<v Zoe Chace>那些竞选进行得怎么样了？

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:20.930
<v Todd Gillman>I think they're going good.
<v Todd Gillman>我觉得进行得很顺利。

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:21.090
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah?
<v Zoe Chace>是吗？

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:21.720
<v Todd Gillman>Yeah.
<v Todd Gillman>是的。

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:37.840
<v Zoe Chace>Todd has never heard of Sean O'Brien and does not care about him, it seems. All he knows about Teamsters is, where did the mafia bury Jimmy Hoffa's body? We're waiting for O'Brien's spot in the lineup when, suddenly, an unannounced guest appears.
<v Zoe Chace>托德似乎从没听说过肖恩·奥布莱恩，也不关心他。他所知道的关于卡车司机工会的一切就是：黑手党把吉米·霍法的尸体埋在哪里了？我们正等着奥布莱恩上台，突然，一位未宣布的嘉宾出现了。

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:40.320
<v Zoe Chace>Oh, Trump's here.
<v Zoe Chace>哦，特朗普来了。

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.330
<v Todd Gillman>He's in the building.
<v Todd Gillman>他在楼里。

00:08:42.330 --> 00:09:03.710
<v Zoe Chace>Trump himself, not scheduled to speak, shows up on screen with a dramatically bandaged ear. It's the first night of the convention, our first time seeing him, and his first time in public since the assassination attempt. The crowd is losing it, the cameras following him down the hall toward the convention room like he's a fighter headed into the ring.
<v Zoe Chace>特朗普本人，原本没有安排发言，出现在屏幕上，耳朵上贴着巨大的绷带。这是大会的第一晚，我们第一次见到他，也是他自暗杀未遂后首次公开露面。人群沸腾了，摄像机跟着他穿过大厅走向会场，就像他是一名走向拳击台的斗士。

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:05.250
<v Zoe Chace>Oh, look at his ear.
<v Zoe Chace>哦，看他的耳朵。

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:13.920
<v Todd Gillman>He might be coming out. Your Teamster guy might not be talking.
<v Todd Gillman>他可能会出来。你的卡车司机那哥们可能没机会讲话了。

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:22.780
<v Zoe Chace>The crowd is jonesing for Trump, but Trump does not take the stage. He sits in the audience. And instead, the guy who appears on stage is--
<v Zoe Chace>人群渴望着特朗普，但特朗普并没有走上舞台。他坐在观众席中。取而代之的是，出现在舞台上的人是——

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:24.120
<v Todd Gillman>Lee Greenwood?
<v Todd Gillman>李·格林伍德（Lee Greenwood）？

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:26.580
<v Zoe Chace>Country singer.
<v Zoe Chace>乡村歌手。

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.980
<v Todd Gillman>He's going to play "Proud to Be an American." And then--
<v Todd Gillman>他要唱《以身为美国人为荣》（Proud to Be an American）。然后——

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:30.810
<v Zoe Chace>Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
<v Zoe Chace>哦，是的，是的，是的。

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:51.580
<v Ira Glass>"Proud to Be an American" is Trump's theme song this campaign. It's generally a signal he's about to take the stage. Only this time, the singer himself is here live, giving a sort of impromptu prayer benediction as images of Trump waving and grimacing appear on screen, turning the white-swaddled ear from side to side.
<v Ira Glass>《以身为美国人为荣》是特朗普这次竞选的主题曲。通常这标志着他即将上台。只不过这次，歌手本人就在现场，给了一种即兴的祈祷式祝福，屏幕上不断出现特朗普挥手和做鬼脸的画面，让那只缠着白绷带的耳朵转来转去。

00:09:51.580 --> 00:10:01.320
<v Lee Greenwood>As Donald Trump turned his head just slightly that the bullet missed him just enough to save his life to be the next president of the United States.
<v Lee Greenwood>当唐纳德·特朗普稍微转动头部，子弹就差那么一点没击中他，救了他的命，让他成为下一任美国总统。

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:05.990
<v Zoe Chace>The crowd sings "Proud to Be an American" with enthusiasm, then chants for Trump.
<v Zoe Chace>人群热情地唱着《以身为美国人为荣》，然后高呼特朗普的名字。

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:09.770
<v Zoe Chace>[CHANTING "WE WANT TRUMP"]
<v Zoe Chace>（高呼“我们要特朗普”）

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:42.610
<v Zoe Chace>But Trump still does not take the stage. Instead, this random guy limps out and stands there, one of the many regular Americans on the program who feel hurt by Biden's policies. He's dubbed onscreen as a grandfather. You've got to feel for him. The crowd is chanting, we want Trump, right into his face. Then another rando with the same story of not being able to afford stuff. Then Amber Rose-- how to describe-- model and influencer talks about seeing the light and voting Republican.
<v Zoe Chace>但特朗普仍然没有上台。取而代之的是，这个随机出现的家伙一瘸一拐地走出来站在那儿，他是节目中许多对拜登政策感到不满的普通美国人之一。他在屏幕上被称为“一位祖父”。你不得不为他感到同情。人群直接对着他的脸高呼“我们要特朗普”。然后是另一个有着同样无法负担生活开支故事的普通人。然后是安珀·罗丝（Amber Rose）——怎么描述呢——模特和网红，讲述自己看到真相并投票给共和党。

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:45.440
<v Todd Gillman>Oh, she does have a forehead tattoo.
<v Todd Gillman>哦，她确实有个额头纹身。

00:10:45.440 --> 00:11:02.480
<v Zoe Chace>Overall, the vibes are way off. Trump's still in the room. People are clamoring for him to speak. And then finally, the last speaker of the night, instead of Trump, it's the president of the Teamsters Union, the outsider guy, the person who doesn't necessarily belong here.
<v Zoe Chace>总的来说，气氛非常不对劲。特朗普还在房间里，人们强烈要求他讲话。最后，当晚最后一位发言者不是特朗普，而是卡车司机工会主席，那个局外人，那个本来不一定属于这里的人。

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:04.010
<v Todd Gillman>Is this the mob boss?
<v Todd Gillman>这就是黑帮老大吗？

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:04.820
<v Zoe Chace>There he is.
<v Zoe Chace>他在那儿。

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:05.820
<v Todd Gillman>This is the mob boss?
<v Todd Gillman>这就是黑帮老大？

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:08.280
<v Zoe Chace>All right, now, you pay attention. Tell me what you think.
<v Zoe Chace>好了，现在，注意听。告诉我你怎么看。

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:11.670
<v Todd Gillman>He's probably a mob boss if he's a Teamsters president.
<v Todd Gillman>如果他是卡车司机工会主席，他可能就是个黑帮老大。

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:16.650
<v Zoe Chace>Clearly, O'Brien has some assumptions about union bosses to overcome with this crowd.
<v Zoe Chace>很明显，奥布莱恩在这个人群中需要克服一些关于工会大佬的假设。

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:26.500
<v Sean O'Brien>Greetings, delegates and guests. I'm Sean O'Brien, general president of the International Brotherhood of Teamsters.
<v Sean O'Brien>各位代表和来宾，大家好。我是国际卡车司机兄弟会总主席肖恩·奥布莱恩。

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:29.720
<v Zoe Chace>OK, big applause. And it just gets bigger.
<v Zoe Chace>OK，掌声雷动。而且掌声越来越大。

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:47.950
<v Sean O'Brien>And I think we all can agree, whether people like him or they don't like him, in light of what happened to him on Saturday, he has proven to be one tough SOB.
<v Sean O'Brien>我想我们都能同意，无论人们喜欢他还是不喜欢他，鉴于周六发生的事情，他已经证明了他是一个坚强的硬汉。

00:11:47.950 --> 00:12:08.040
<v Zoe Chace>Biggest applause line of the night by far, like the catharsis of waiting for Trump is satisfied by this, a line that Sean will tell me he came up with right then, wasn't on the teleprompter. He was just feeling it. My Michigan delegate, Todd, is cheering, along with his military buddy, Bob, from Wisconsin. Bob leans over.
<v Zoe Chace>这是当晚最响亮的掌声，就像等待特朗普的宣泄通过这一点得到了满足。肖恩后来告诉我，这一句是他临时想到的，提词器上没有。他只是有感而发。我的密歇根代表托德正和他的威斯康星州战友鲍勃一起欢呼。鲍勃凑过来。

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:22.260
<v Bob>This is huge. I mean, you've got all these different people that voted for Joe Biden-- and their families did-- coming out here and speaking. This could turn out to be a total butt-kicking in November.
<v Bob>这太厉害了。我是说，你让这些曾经投票给乔·拜登的人——以及他们的家人——都站出来发言。这可能会导致十一月份的一场彻底惨败。

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:49.330
<v Zoe Chace>Now, the Teamsters are not endorsing Trump tonight. Sean O'Brien has been quite clear about that. But Bob from Wisconsin is just hearing the Teamsters throw their weight behind Trump. He's not paying close attention to the specifics of what Sean is saying, really. And as it goes on, the speech does get much more specific and much less Republican Convention-sounding, basically. He throws long-standing Republican allies, mainstays of the party, under the bus.
<v Zoe Chace>现在，卡车司机工会今晚并没有背书特朗普。肖恩·奥布莱恩对此表达得很清楚。但来自威斯康星州的鲍勃听到的只是卡车司机工会全力支持特朗普。他并没有真正关注肖恩所说内容的细节。随着演讲的深入，演讲内容变得越来越具体，也越来越不像共和党大会的风格。他实际上把共和党的老盟友、党的支柱都抛弃了。

00:12:49.330 --> 00:13:07.330
<v Sean O'Brien>But for a century, major employers have waged a war against labor by forming corporate unions of their own. We need to call the Chamber of Commerce and the business roundtables what they are. They are unions for big business. And here's another fact.
<v Sean O'Brien>但一个世纪以来，大雇主们通过组建自己的企业工会，对劳工发动了一场战争。我们需要把商会和商业圆桌会议叫出原形。他们是大企业的工会。这里还有一个事实。

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:08.960
<v Zoe Chace>You think that's right?
<v Zoe Chace>你觉得对吗？

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:14.710
<v Todd Gillman>Yeah, he's right. I never thought of it that way, but he's 100% correct.
<v Todd Gillman>是的，他是对的。我从来没这么想过，但他百分之百正确。

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:25.010
<v Zoe Chace>He talks about major American companies, not as great examples of entrepreneurship, like at a typical Republican Convention, but as great examples of corporate greed.
<v Zoe Chace>他谈论大型美国公司时，不是像典型的共和党大会那样把它们作为创业的伟大典范，而是作为“企业贪婪”的伟大典范。

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:42.410
<v Sean O'Brien>Companies like Amazon are bigger than most national economies. Amazon is valued over $2 trillion. What is sickening is that Amazon has abandoned any national allegiance. Amazon's sole focus is on lining its own pockets.
<v Sean O'Brien>像亚马逊这样的公司比大多数国家经济体还要大。亚马逊的估值超过2万亿美元。令人作呕的是，亚马逊已经放弃了任何国家忠诚。亚马逊唯一的重点就是填满自己的腰包。

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:52.770
<v Zoe Chace>People are like, am I with this? Companies are bad? It's a little awkward. But then O'Brien pulls out a magic word that sells in this room.
<v Zoe Chace>人们心想：我支持这个吗？公司是坏的？这有点尴尬。但随后奥布莱恩抛出了一个在这个房间里很受欢迎的关键词。

00:13:52.770 --> 00:14:05.460
<v Sean O'Brien>Remember, elites have no party. Elites have no nation. Their loyalty is to the balance sheet and the stock price at the expense of the American worker.
<v Sean O'Brien>记住，精英们没有政党。精英们没有国家。他们的忠诚在于资产负债表和股票价格，而牺牲的是美国工人。

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:06.780
<v Todd Gillman>I love this guy.
<v Todd Gillman>我喜欢这个家伙。

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:08.220
<v Bob>I can't stand Amazon.
<v Bob>我无法忍受亚马逊。

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:10.910
<v Todd Gillman>Yeah, I love this guy.
<v Todd Gillman>是的，我喜欢这个家伙。

00:14:10.910 --> 00:14:11.350
<v Bob>Yep.
<v Bob>是的。

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:21.110
<v Zoe Chace>At some point, the crowd seems to hear themselves cheering wildly for organized labor and against corporatists. And it does get a little more strained-feeling in the room.
<v Zoe Chace>在某一刻，人群似乎听见自己在为有组织劳工疯狂欢呼，并反对企业集团。房间里的气氛变得有点紧张。

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:25.870
<v Sean O'Brien>There are no consequences for the company, only the worker.
<v Sean O'Brien>公司没有后果，只有工人有。

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:43.990
<v Zoe Chace>Right to work! Right to work! Someone is heckling the Teamster president kind of wonkily by screaming out the name of a policy that Teamsters hate. Right to work is a shot directly into the heart of unions' ability to organize. It's at the very center of Republican labor policy.
<v Zoe Chace>“工作权利”法（Right to work）！“工作权利”法！有人正通过高喊一个卡车司机工会痛恨的政策名称来刁难工会主席。“工作权利”法直接击中了工会组织能力的核心。这是共和党劳工政策的核心。

00:14:43.990 --> 00:15:10.660
<v Zoe Chace>Right to work allows workers to work in a union shop without having to pay union dues. Advocates of the policy say workers should get to choose what they want. Unions hate it because they say workers then get the benefits of the contract without paying for it. It undercuts their power. There's over 150 Republicans co-sponsoring right-to-work bills in Congress right now. Trump has said he supports right to work.
<v Zoe Chace>“工作权利”法允许工人在工会企业中工作而无需支付工会会费。该政策的支持者说，工人应该能够选择他们想要的。工会讨厌它，因为他们说工人随后获得了合同的好处却不为此付费。这削弱了他们的力量。现在国会中有超过150名共和党人共同发起“工作权利”法案。特朗普也表示支持该法案。

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:20.780
<v Zoe Chace>Sean O'Brien continues to slam big business. And I'm almost expecting him to talk about Bernie's millionaires and billionaires. The applause in the room is getting weaker.
<v Zoe Chace>肖恩·奥布莱恩继续抨击大企业。我几乎以为他要谈论桑德斯的“百万富翁和亿万富翁”了。房间里的掌声变得微弱了。

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:42.100
<v Sean O'Brien>These companies offer no real health insurance, no retirement benefits, no pay leave, relying on underfunded public assistance. And who foots the bill? The individual taxpayer. The biggest recipients of welfare in this country are corporations. And this is real corruption. We must put workers first.
<v Sean O'Brien>这些公司不提供真正的医疗保险，没有退休福利，没有带薪假，依赖于资金不足的公共援助。谁来买单？个人纳税人。这个国家最大的福利接受者是企业。这是真正的腐败。我们必须把工人放在首位。

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:46.540
<v Zoe Chace>By the end of the speech, Todd's feeling about this guy he'd never heard of before--
<v Zoe Chace>演讲快结束时，托德对这个他之前从未听说过的家伙的感受是——

00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:55.750
<v Todd Gillman>He's knocking it out of the park. I'm glad I stuck around and listened to him. Thanks.
<v Todd Gillman>他讲得太棒了。我很高兴我留下来听了他的演讲。谢谢。

00:15:55.750 --> 00:16:11.320
<v Zoe Chace>When I talked to O'Brien after the speech, in the lobby of the grand Pfister Hotel, I wanted to know if he felt what I was feeling, about the awkwardness in the room, the uncertain silence, when he started wailing on the Chamber of Commerce and big American companies.
<v Zoe Chace>当我在大菲斯特酒店大堂采访演讲后的奥布莱恩时，我想知道他是否感受到了我所感受到的那种尴尬，那种当他开始痛斥商会和美国大公司时，房间里的那种不确定性的沉默。

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:14.290
<v Zoe Chace>Did you feel a feeling in the room like this is going over weird?
<v Zoe Chace>你有没有感觉到房间里有种奇怪的感觉？

00:16:14.290 --> 00:16:35.170
<v Sean O'Brien>Not at all. Not at all. I mean, I felt real comfortable. I knew that-- it's like anything else. If you're in a crowd and someone's talking about a situation and you may think you are the subject of it or you may think that pertains to you, it may be a little bit more uncomfortable for them than it was for me.
<v Sean O'Brien>完全没有。完全没有。我的意思是，我感觉很自在。我的意思是，这就像其他事情一样。如果你在人群中，有人正在谈论某种情况，而你认为自己可能是对象，或者你认为这与你有关，那么对他们来说可能比对我来说更不舒服。

00:16:35.170 --> 00:16:54.070
<v Zoe Chace>Overall, he feels pretty great about the speech. He got his message out. He thinks that now, he'll get calls from Republican legislators who weren't meeting with him before. Like, there's this one Senate bill right now that the big unions are really fighting for. It's called the PRO Act, Protecting the Right to Organize Act.
<v Zoe Chace>总的来说，他对自己演讲感觉很好。他传达了他的信息。他认为现在，他会接到之前不愿见他的共和党议员的电话。比如，现在有一项参议院法案，大工会正在为此努力奋斗。它叫《PRO法案》，即《保护组织权利法案》。

00:16:54.070 --> 00:16:58.980
<v Zoe Chace>It's all Democrats. There's not a single Republican signer onto that bill.
<v Zoe Chace>那全是民主党人的事。没有一个共和党人签署该法案。

00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:02.570
<v Sean O'Brien>Not yet. Not yet, but we're working on it.
<v Sean O'Brien>还没签，还没签，但我们正在努力。

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:04.130
<v Zoe Chace>Do you think you got some people--
<v Zoe Chace>你觉得你拉到人了吗——

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:06.380
<v Sean O'Brien>Well, the bill that's currently there.
<v Sean O'Brien>嗯，目前的法案在那里。

00:17:06.380 --> 00:17:06.690
<v Zoe Chace>Right.
<v Zoe Chace>是的。

00:17:06.690 --> 00:17:17.060
<v Sean O'Brien>Right. So if you know you're not going to get support-- it's not going to pass-- wouldn't it make sense to figure out what could pass bipartisan?
<v Sean O'Brien>是的。所以如果你知道你得不到支持——它不会通过——那么找出什么是两党都能通过的不是更有意义吗？

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:21.890
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah. So do you think you got people in that room to reconsider?
<v Zoe Chace>是的。所以你认为你让那个房间里的人重新考虑了吗？

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:23.250
<v Sean O'Brien>I know that for a fact.
<v Sean O'Brien>我确定这一点。

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:24.040
<v Zoe Chace>How do you know?
<v Zoe Chace>你怎么知道？

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:25.940
<v Sean O'Brien>Because I've had the conversations.
<v Sean O'Brien>因为我进行了谈话。

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:27.250
<v Zoe Chace>Can you give me an example?
<v Zoe Chace>你能给我一个例子吗？

00:17:27.250 --> 00:17:29.420
<v Sean O'Brien>I cannot, but stay tuned.
<v Sean O'Brien>我不能，但请继续关注。

00:17:29.420 --> 00:17:41.420
<v Zoe Chace>Now, the very next day, the Teamsters announced there was a new co-sponsor of the House version of the bill, a third Republican House member from Oregon. Because of the speech? We don't know.
<v Zoe Chace>第二天，卡车司机工会宣布该法案的众议院版本有了新的共同发起人，那是来自俄勒冈州的第三位共和党众议员。是因为这次演讲吗？我们不知道。

00:17:41.420 --> 00:17:50.630
<v Zoe Chace>Rick Smith, the 35-year Teamster with the radio show, watched the convention, watched the speech from his union president, and I called him afterwards.
<v Zoe Chace>里克·史密斯，那位拥有广播节目的35年工龄卡车司机，观看了大会，观看了工会主席的演讲。我事后给他打了电话。

00:17:50.630 --> 00:18:06.950
<v Rick Smith>I fear he did everything that I thought he was going to do, because, look, I don't think anybody remembers what he says tomorrow other than, Trump's a tough SOB. There may be a quote, but what they're going to remember is he was there for Donald Trump.
<v Rick Smith>我担心他做了我所想的一切，因为看，除了“特朗普是个坚强的硬汉”，没人会记得他说了什么。可能会有一句引语，但他们会记得的是他当时站在唐纳德·特朗普那边。

00:18:06.950 --> 00:18:31.280
<v Zoe Chace>Rick saw Sean's speech as just another piece of the political theater playing out at the RNC, more working man cosplay, like Trump's selection of JD Vance as vice president, in his opinion. Sean O'Brien praised Vance in his speech. Vance's brand is kind of made-in-America economic populism. He famously walked a UAW picket line in Toledo.
<v Zoe Chace>里克认为肖恩的演讲只是在共和党全代会上上演的政治表演的另一部分，更多的“蓝领工人装扮”，就像特朗普选择万斯作为副总统一样。肖恩·奥布莱恩在演讲中赞扬了万斯。万斯的品牌某种程度上是“美国制造的经济民粹主义”。他曾因在托莱多参加UAW罢工而闻名。

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:51.850
<v Rick Smith>I go back to this idea that there are Republicans that support labor. JD Vance walked a picket line. Yeah, OK, he walked the picket line. But he doesn't support workers' right to organize. And I know he'll give on something here or there. But ultimately, this is a guy who's not a friend of labor.
<v Rick Smith>我回到这个想法，即有支持劳工的共和党人。万斯参加了罢工。是的，好吧，他参加了罢工。但他不支持工人的组织权利。我知道他会在某处让步。但最终，这家伙不是劳工的朋友。

00:18:51.850 --> 00:19:09.920
<v Rick Smith>And it seems simple to me. These are the people who are going to put the final nail in the coffin of the labor movement. And I think he gave them the kind of fodder that they can use. It's like handing an ax murderer an ax and then being surprised. What does Trump always say about a snake biting you because a snake is a snake, right?
<v Rick Smith>对我来说，这很简单。这些人将成为劳工运动棺材上的最后一颗钉子。我认为他给了他们可以利用的筹码。这就像把斧头交给杀人犯，然后表现得很惊讶。特朗普总是说什么关于蛇咬你，因为蛇就是蛇，对吧？

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:12.620
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah, it's the snake's nature.
<v Zoe Chace>是的，这是蛇的本性。

00:19:12.620 --> 00:19:16.900
<v Rick Smith>Right, and this is-- that's these people's nature.
<v Rick Smith>对，这就是这些人的本性。

00:19:16.900 --> 00:19:27.550
<v Zoe Chace>O'Brien's response is, maybe the snake is changing. We can make them change. Parties change. You can't get things done if that's your uncompromising approach.
<v Zoe Chace>奥布莱恩的回应是，也许蛇正在改变。我们可以让他们改变。政党会改变。如果那是你不妥协的方法，你就什么都做不成。

00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:39.620
<v Sean O'Brien>I mean, look, you look at the Democratic Party 20 years ago as compared to now, I think we can all agree there's been significant change. Conversely, you look at the Republican Party 20 years ago and look at it today, I mean, there's been significant change.
<v Sean O'Brien>我的意思是，看看民主党20年前与现在的对比，我想我们都能同意发生了重大的变化。相反，看看共和党20年前和今天，我的意思是，发生了重大的变化。

00:19:39.620 --> 00:19:49.170
<v Zoe Chace>But you don't have to go back 20 years in this case, with this election. You can look at the Biden administration and the Trump administration. And one is more pro-labor than the other.
<v Zoe Chace>但在这次选举中，你不需要回到20年前。你可以看看拜登政府和特朗普政府。一个比另一个更支持劳工。

00:19:49.170 --> 00:20:14.960
<v Sean O'Brien>There's no doubt about that. And I can speak to that all day long. I mean, President Biden fixed pensions, brought a lot of work, passed the CHIPS Act, infrastructure bill, all that stuff. There's no doubt. But that doesn't mean that we can't work with anybody else that doesn't have the same labor record. And maybe they can-- obviously, if they have the opportunity, they can do good things for working people as well.
<v Sean O'Brien>毫无疑问。我可以整天谈论这个。我的意思是，拜登总统修复了养老金，带来了大量工作，通过了《芯片法案》、基础设施法案等等。毫无疑问。但这并不意味着我们不能与任何其他没有同样劳工记录的人合作。也许他们可以——显然，如果有机会，他们也可以为劳动人民做些好事。

00:20:14.960 --> 00:20:50.410
<v Zoe Chace>This sounds either wickedly pragmatic or hopelessly naive. But however much the Democrats and Joe Biden have done for organized labor, the Democrats could definitely lose in November. And Trump, who was sitting right across the hall from O'Brien up on stage performing, watching him closely with that martyred ear, he might be the guy he'll have to negotiate with.
<v Zoe Chace>这听起来要么是极度务实的，要么是无可救药的天真。但无论民主党和乔·拜登为有组织劳工做了多少，民主党确实可能在十一月失败。而那个当时坐在奥布莱恩对面舞台上表演、用那种受难的耳朵紧盯着他的特朗普，可能就是他不得不谈判的对象。

00:20:50.410 --> 00:20:53.680
<v Ira Glass>Zoe Chace is a producer on our show.
<v Ira Glass>佐伊·蔡斯是我们节目的制作人。

00:20:53.680 --> 00:21:07.510
<v Ira Glass>Act two, Out on a Limb. You know who July hasn't been so great for? The Democratic politicians who were out front before anybody, publicly calling for President Biden to drop out of the election.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，孤注一掷。你知道七月对谁来说不太好过吗？那些在任何人之前就公开呼吁拜登总统退选的民主党政客。

00:21:07.510 --> 00:21:37.210
<v Ira Glass>Out of 260 Democrats who sit in the House and Senate, only 21 stepped forward, 20 from the House of Representatives and one senator, for the first three weeks after the presidential debate. No Democratic governors. No former presidents. But this small group called for Biden to drop out, hoping an army was going to form behind them. Then a week passed, then another week, and the battalions did not show up. They hung out there by themselves.
<v Ira Glass>在参众两院的260名民主党人中，只有21人挺身而出，20名众议员和1名参议员，那是总统辩论后的最初三周。没有民主党州长，没有前总统。但这一小群人呼吁拜登退选，希望军队能在这场运动后面形成。然后一周过去了，又一周，营队没有出现。他们孤零零地站在那里。

00:21:37.210 --> 00:22:00.180
<v Ira Glass>Finally, at the end of this week, we heard about big names in the party, Pelosi, Schumer, Jeffries, behind the scenes, urging the president to step aside. With that cover, going into the weekend, the number of elected lawmakers started edging up very slowly. And finally, of course, Joe Biden bowed out of the race. But it took over three weeks for things to get to this point.
<v Ira Glass>终于，在本周结束时，我们听到了党内大佬佩洛西、舒默、杰弗里斯在幕后敦促总统退选。有了这个保障，进入周末，当选议员的人数开始缓慢上升。当然，乔·拜登最终退出了竞选。但事情走到这一步花了超过三周的时间。

00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:21.380
<v Ira Glass>So what was it like for the people who stuck their necks out and waited? Well, Congressman Seth Moulton agreed to talk to me about all that. He's a Democrat from Massachusetts, a rising star in the party, had a brief presidential run himself in 2020, a former Marine who served four tours in Iraq. In fact, he led a platoon in the first company to enter Baghdad.
<v Ira Glass>那么对于那些挺身而出并等待的人来说，那是什么样的经历呢？国会议员塞斯·莫尔顿（Seth Moulton）同意与我谈谈这一切。他是来自马萨诸塞州的民主党人，党内的一颗冉冉升起的新星，2020年曾短暂竞选总统，一名前海军陆战队员，在伊拉克服役了四个任期。事实上，他率领了第一家进入巴格达的连队中的一个排。

00:22:21.380 --> 00:22:40.400
<v Ira Glass>Years ago, he did a memorable interview on our program about this interpreter who he worked with and got close to in Iraq who ended up coming to the States and living in Moulton's childhood home with his parents. Basically, he became part of their family while waiting to get asylum because of all the death threats against him for collaborating with Americans.
<v Ira Glass>多年前，他在我们的节目中做过一次难忘的采访，关于他在伊拉克共事并结下深厚友谊的一名翻译。那个人最终来到了美国，住进了莫尔顿父母的家里。基本上，他成为了他们家庭的一部分，同时因为与美国人合作而面临的所有死亡威胁，一直在等待庇护。

00:22:40.400 --> 00:23:00.320
<v Ira Glass>Joe Biden took an interest in Seth Moulton back when Moulton first ran for Congress in 2014, when Biden was vice president. Biden went to Massachusetts to do a rally endorsing him. And they rode in a car together, where Moulton says the vice president offered him half of his peanut butter and jelly sandwich. And he was not sure if you're supposed to say no to that offer from the vice president. So he said yes.
<v Ira Glass>乔·拜登在2014年莫尔顿首次竞选国会议员时就对他产生了兴趣，当时拜登还是副总统。拜登去马萨诸塞州参加集会支持他。他们坐在同一辆车里，莫尔顿说副总统给了他一半的花生酱果酱三明治。他不确定是否应该拒绝副总统的提议，所以他接受了。

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:16.880
<v Ira Glass>Moulton's age and his military experience, Moulton says Biden's told him a few times he reminds him of his son, Beau. Moulton and I spoke this past Monday, July 15. He told me the story of turning his back on Joe Biden starts, of course, on debate night.
<v Ira Glass>莫尔顿的年龄和他的军事经历，莫尔顿说拜登曾几次告诉他，他让他想起了自己的儿子博（Beau）。我和莫尔顿在上周一，7月15日进行了交谈。他告诉我，背弃乔·拜登的故事确实是从辩论之夜开始的。

00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:45.120
<v Seth Moulton>Well, I think, like most Americans, I felt the debate was an unmitigated disaster for President Biden. I mean, we all remember the times he couldn't keep his train of thought. He lost his place. He seemed incoherent. I thought one of the most remarkable moments was when he literally took our best issue, abortion, and pivoted immediately to our worst issue, immigration. I mean, you almost couldn't make it up.
<v Seth Moulton>嗯，我想像大多数美国人一样，我觉得那场辩论对拜登总统来说是一场彻底的灾难。我的意思是，我们都记得他无法保持思路连贯的时刻。他忘记了自己说到哪儿了。他看起来语无伦次。我认为最引人注目的时刻之一是他直接拿走了我们最好的议题——堕胎，并立即转向我们最糟糕的议题——移民。我的意思是，你简直编都编不出来。

00:23:45.120 --> 00:24:05.760
<v Ira Glass>I want to ask you a question that I worry might be a little awkward for you to answer. And I hope you can answer it honestly. The side of President Biden that we all saw on television that night during the debate-- you're in DC. You hear a lot of things, I'm sure. Had you seen that side of him before that night? Had you heard about that side of him before that night? Is that something people talk about?
<v Ira Glass>我想问你一个问题，我担心你回答起来可能有点尴尬。我希望你能诚实地回答。那天晚上辩论期间我们在电视上看到的拜登总统的那一面——你在华盛顿，我相信你听到了很多事情。在那天晚上之前你见过他那样子吗？在那天晚上之前你听说过他那样子吗？这是人们谈论的事情吗？

00:24:05.760 --> 00:24:42.720
<v Seth Moulton>Yes. Yes, it is. I've seen the president fairly regularly as a member of Congress, although it does feel like his staff is hiding him far more than they used to. But I've seen a dramatic change, in particular over the last several months. I saw him at the White House Christmas Ball. And he looked older and frailer. But he was excited to see me, quickly recounted things we've done together or whatnot.
<v Seth Moulton>是的。是的，确实。作为国会议员，我相当经常见到总统，尽管感觉他的工作人员比以前更隐瞒他了。但我确实看到了一个巨大的变化，特别是在过去几个月里。我在白宫圣诞舞会上见到他。他看起来更老更虚弱。但他很高兴见到我，很快地复述了我们一起做过的事情之类的。

00:24:42.720 --> 00:25:00.080
<v Seth Moulton>Then I saw him, most recently, just a few weeks ago in Normandy, for the 80th anniversary of D-Day. He was speaking with a small group of us. And the change was pretty dramatic.
<v Seth Moulton>然后我最近，就在几周前在诺曼底，为了诺曼底登陆80周年纪念日见到了他。他和我们的一小群人说话。这种变化非常巨大。

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:01.840
<v Ira Glass>How so?
<v Ira Glass>怎么说？

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:09.970
<v Seth Moulton>Well, it was more like the things that we saw in the debate. Not that bad, but more in that direction.
<v Seth Moulton>嗯，更像是我们在辩论中看到的那些。没有那么糟，但更朝那个方向发展。

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:15.400
<v Ira Glass>Like him having a hard time summoning the words for things?
<v Ira Glass>比如他很难用词来表达事物？

00:25:15.400 --> 00:25:45.640
<v Seth Moulton>Yeah, more like that, yes. But as someone who's been a very long-time Joe Biden fan, as someone who's admired him, as someone who's been a mentee of his, it was hard to see. And it was concerning, to the point where, although I don't think there's a single American who expected what we saw the night of that debate, I wasn't entirely surprised.
<v Seth Moulton>是的，更像那样，是的。但作为一个长期以来的乔·拜登粉丝，作为一个钦佩他的人，作为一个他的门生，这很难看。而且这令人担忧，以至于虽然我认为没有一个美国人预料到我们在辩论那晚看到的情况，但我并不完全感到惊讶。

00:25:45.640 --> 00:26:03.880
<v Seth Moulton>So when we came back to the House floor the next day, everyone was sharing the same view. I didn't speak to anyone who thought it was OK other than Speaker Pelosi, who just said, well, I don't know that we can judge a campaign by one debate. But I really felt she had to say that.
<v Seth Moulton>所以第二天当我们回到国会大厅时，大家的看法是一样的。我没跟任何觉得这没问题的人谈过，除了佩洛西议长，她只是说，好吧，我不知道我们能否通过一场辩论来判断一场竞选。但我真的觉得她必须这么说。

00:26:03.880 --> 00:26:20.490
<v Seth Moulton>Party leaders are in a little bit different position. But all of us in the rank and file thought it was a total disaster. And I think most of us were clear that the only way forward for Democrats to have a chance in this election is to get a new nominee at the top of the ticket.
<v Seth Moulton>党内领导人的处境有点不同。但我们这些普通议员都认为这是一场彻底的灾难。而且我认为我们大多数人都清楚，民主党在这场选举中有机会的前进道路，就是获得一个新的提名人。

00:26:20.490 --> 00:26:30.720
<v Ira Glass>Over the next few days, Moulton says, he tried to call everybody he knew in the White House, anybody close to the inner circle, to express his concerns about the president's performance and the path ahead.
<v Ira Glass>在接下来的几天里，莫尔顿说，他试图给他在白宫认识的每一个人、任何接近核心圈子的人打电话，以表达他对总统表现和未来道路的担忧。

00:26:30.720 --> 00:26:53.370
<v Seth Moulton>I made a lot of phone calls. And I got nothing in response. Now, I'm not offended by that. I don't want to come across as thinking that the White House should return my phone calls. But given the circumstances, you'd expect some low-level aide to just give me a call and say, we've heard your concerns. Here's what we're doing about them. But it was just a wall of silence.
<v Seth Moulton>我打了很多电话。但我没有得到任何回应。现在，我并不因此感到冒犯。我不想让别人觉得我以为白宫应该回我的电话。但鉴于这种情况，你会期望某个低级助手给我打个电话说，我们听到了你的担忧。这就是我们正在做的。但那只是一堵沉默之墙。

00:26:53.370 --> 00:26:56.020
<v Ira Glass>What did that say to you?
<v Ira Glass>那说明了什么？

00:26:56.020 --> 00:27:11.910
<v Seth Moulton>It said to me that they're not taking this seriously. We needed to hear a new plan. And first, you just simply need to hear that they're being honest about the situation at hand. But I wasn't hearing any of that.
<v Seth Moulton>这说明他们没有认真对待这件事。我们需要听到新的计划。首先，你需要听到的是他们对目前的情况表现得诚实。但我根本没听到这些。

00:27:11.910 --> 00:27:39.520
<v Seth Moulton>When I came home to Massachusetts, I held a Zoom meeting with a lot of local Democratic officials. And these are people who tend to be very establishment players. They want to stick with the status quo, by and large. So I expected the majority of them to be in favor of Biden remaining on the ticket. I'd say it was about 80% in favor of his stepping aside. That was powerful as well.
<v Seth Moulton>当我回到马萨诸塞州时，我和许多当地民主党官员举行了Zoom会议。这些人倾向于非常建制派。他们想要坚持现状。所以我预计他们中的大多数人会支持拜登留任。我得说大约80%支持他退选。那也很强大。

00:27:39.520 --> 00:28:09.500
<v Ira Glass>It's five days after the debate before the first Democratic lawmaker calls for Biden to step down. A day later, a second one does. That same night, Moulton releases a very polite, pretty hedgey statement saying, "I have grave concerns about President Biden's ability to defeat Donald Trump," and then going on to say if the president can't demonstrate that he can do a better job prosecuting the case against Trump, quote, "We should have all viable options on the table." So that goes out.
<v Ira Glass>辩论五天后，第一位民主党议员呼吁拜登退选。一天后，第二位也这么做了。就在当晚，莫尔顿发表了一份非常有礼貌、相当回避的声明，说：“我对拜登总统击败唐纳德·特朗普的能力深表关切，”然后继续说，如果总统不能证明他能更好地起诉针对特朗普的案件，引语，“我们应该把所有可行的选择都摆在桌面上。”所以这发出去了。

00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:31.090
<v Seth Moulton>But then later that night, I showed my statement to a friend who was visiting, a Marine. And this great friend of mine, who I trust for advice, he read it, and he said, what are you trying to say? I said, well, I'm basically saying that he should step aside.
<v Seth Moulton>但后来当晚，我给一位正在探访我的朋友看我的声明，那是一名海军陆战队员。我信任他的建议，他读了之后问，你到底想说什么？我说，好吧，我基本上是说他应该退选。

00:28:31.090 --> 00:28:54.220
<v Seth Moulton>And he said, well, then why don't you just say it, Seth? And I gave him this long explanation for why being polite was the appropriate thing to do here and actually more likely to result in mission accomplishment. Let's be respectful of the president and push him gently in the right position, not be so explicit.
<v Seth Moulton>他说，那为什么不直接说出来呢，塞斯？我给了他一个长篇大论，解释为什么有礼貌是这里的适当做法，实际上更有可能实现任务成就。让我们尊重总统，轻轻地把他推向正确的位置，而不是那么直接。

00:28:54.220 --> 00:29:11.620
<v Seth Moulton>But I thought about it a lot that night. And I woke up thinking about it the next morning. And I decided, if I honestly think he should step aside, then you know what? I should just say it. And so the next morning on the one interview I did, a local NPR station--
<v Seth Moulton>但我那天晚上想了很多。第二天早上醒来时我还在想。我决定，如果我真心认为他应该退选，那么你知道吗？我应该直接说出来。所以第二天早上我接受的唯一采访，在当地一家NPR电台——

00:29:11.620 --> 00:29:12.790
<v Ira Glass>WBUR.
<v Ira Glass>WBUR。

00:29:12.790 --> 00:29:20.170
<v Seth Moulton>When we were talking about the statement, I just simply added those few words. He should step aside.
<v Seth Moulton>当我们谈论那个声明时，我只是简单地加了那几个字：他应该退选。

00:29:20.170 --> 00:29:40.090
<v Ira Glass>And with that, Seth Moulton became the third Democrat to demand the president step down. He told me he didn't expect tons of his colleagues to join him and follow suit, even though so many of them agreed that Biden should step aside. That's just how Congress works, he says. He's in other situations where his colleagues agree on important stuff behind the scenes but don't take a public stand.
<v Ira Glass>就这样，塞斯·莫尔顿成为第三位要求总统退选的民主党人。他告诉我，他没指望他的同事们会成群结队地加入他，效仿他，尽管他们中许多人都同意拜登应该退选。他说，这就是国会的工作方式。他处在其他情况下，他的同事们在幕后对重要的事情意见一致，但不会公开立场。

00:29:40.090 --> 00:29:50.220
<v Seth Moulton>I didn't, frankly, expect a lot of people to jump right in with me. But I mean, I'm sorry to admit that.
<v Seth Moulton>坦率地说，我没指望很多人会立刻加入我。但我的意思是，我很抱歉承认这一点。

00:29:50.220 --> 00:30:05.800
<v Ira Glass>And so what happened? Watching the news, I really wondered if, for somebody like you, if after making a statement like that-- you're walking through the halls of Congress. You're going down to the floor. Are you, like, a pariah? Are people not meeting your eye? What's it like?
<v Ira Glass>那么发生了什么？看着新闻，我真的很想知道，对于像你这样的人，在发表了那样的声明之后——你走在国会大厅里。你走到会场。你像是个贱民吗？人们避开你的目光吗？那是种什么样的经历？

00:30:05.800 --> 00:30:35.690
<v Seth Moulton>Well, it was interesting. I was very curious what would happen the first time I came back to Washington and walked out onto the House floor for votes that evening. And it did seem like people were just avoiding me, like they didn't want to be caught in a conversation. But it was totally different as soon as I walked into the cloakroom, in other words, away from the reporters and the TV cameras. And people had a lot of nice things to say.
<v Seth Moulton>嗯，这很有趣。我很好奇第一次回到华盛顿走上国会大厅投票时会发生什么。看起来人们确实在避开我，好像他们不想陷入对话。但一旦我走进衣帽间，换句话说，远离了记者和电视摄像机，情况就完全不同了。人们有很多好话要说。

00:30:35.690 --> 00:30:56.020
<v Ira Glass>He says he did try to lobby some of his colleagues, told him they should come forward publicly also. And a few did. But generally, most did not. And why not? Fear, best as he can tell. They were scared of ticking off the president and his team, of going against the Democratic establishment, of sticking their necks out.
<v Ira Glass>他说他确实试图游说他的一些同事，告诉他们也应该公开站出来。有几个人做了。但总的来说，大多数人没有。为什么不呢？他能确定的只有恐惧。他们害怕惹恼总统和他的团队，害怕违背民主党建制派，害怕挺身而出。

00:30:56.020 --> 00:31:13.170
<v Ira Glass>That fear is not entirely unreasonable. Michigan Congresswoman Hillary Scholten was kicked out of a Democratic Party planning meeting to coordinate get-out-the-vote efforts because, Politico reported, the Michigan State Democratic Party didn't like the fact that she called on President Biden to step down.
<v Ira Glass>这种恐惧并非完全不合理。密歇根州女众议员希拉里·肖尔滕（Hillary Scholten）被踢出了一个民主党规划会议，该会议旨在协调投票工作，因为Politico报道称，密歇根州民主党不喜欢她呼吁拜登总统退选。

00:31:13.170 --> 00:31:25.780
<v Ira Glass>Moulton told me the main time he heard anybody make any kind of principled argument against speaking out was at a meeting of the Democratic caucus. This is Democratic members of the House. They met 12 days after the debate to discuss Biden.
<v Ira Glass>莫尔顿告诉我，他听到有人反对公开站出来发表原则性论点的主要时间是在民主党核心小组会议上。这是民主党众议院议员。他们在辩论后12天开会讨论拜登。

00:31:25.780 --> 00:31:44.100
<v Seth Moulton>People who were concerned about the chaos or supposed chaos of what might come next, the people who were unsure whether this should go directly to Vice President Harris or should be some sort of open mini-primary, as James Carville and Jim Clyburn have suggested.
<v Seth Moulton>那些担心混乱或所谓接下来可能出现的混乱的人，那些不确定是否应该直接传给哈里斯副总统，还是应该进行某种詹姆斯·卡维尔（James Carville）和吉姆·克莱伯恩（Jim Clyburn）建议的某种开放式迷你初选的人。

00:31:44.100 --> 00:31:54.870
<v Ira Glass>Moulton spoke at that meeting. And afterwards, he says, colleagues who were not sticking their necks out publicly continued to tell him that they liked what he was saying and doing in public.
<v Ira Glass>莫尔顿在会议上发言。事后他说，没有公开站出来的同事继续告诉他，他们喜欢他在公开场合所说和所做的一切。

00:31:54.870 --> 00:31:59.630
<v Ira Glass>Do you feel impatient or frustrated with them?
<v Ira Glass>你对他们感到不耐烦或沮丧吗？

00:31:59.630 --> 00:32:17.800
<v Seth Moulton>Yes. Yes. I mean, I'll be honest. Look, I know people have very different political backgrounds and constituencies to represent. I know some people are hearing more folks back home who just want Biden to stay in the race. At least, I'm guessing that they do.
<v Seth Moulton>是的。是的。我的意思是，我会诚实地说。听着，我知道人们有着非常不同的政治背景和需要代表的选区。我知道有些人在家乡听到了更多只是想让拜登留在竞选中的人的声音。至少，我猜他们是这样。

00:32:17.800 --> 00:32:35.820
<v Ira Glass>And what is your read of the politics of this? Like, you're in a safe Democratic district or a pretty safe Democratic district. Is this good for you politically? Like, calls and emails and texts and stuff to your office, are they mostly people supporting you, coming out and saying this about the president?
<v Ira Glass>你对这个政治局势有什么看法？就像，你在一个安全的民主党选区或一个相当安全的民主党选区。这对你政治上有利吗？就像，给你们办公室的电话、邮件和短信之类的，大多是支持你的人，站出来支持你对总统的这种说法吗？

00:32:35.820 --> 00:33:00.200
<v Seth Moulton>Interestingly, the calls and messages we've received from people in the district are very supportive. It's just, thank you for saying what we all saw. But there seems to be some kind of organized campaign nationally to attack me, because a lot of the messages on social media, on Twitter, and to our office that come from outside the district have been highly critical.
<v Seth Moulton>有趣的是，我们从选区收到的电话和信息非常支持。只是，谢谢你说了我们大家所看到的一切。但全国范围内似乎有一种有组织的运动来攻击我，因为社交媒体上、Twitter上发给我们的信息，以及从选区外发来的信息都非常尖刻。

00:33:00.200 --> 00:33:09.170
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait. Do you think it's possible that your own party or your own president is organizing people to yell at you online?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等。你认为你的政党或你自己的总统有可能在组织人们在线上骂你吗？

00:33:09.170 --> 00:33:21.440
<v Seth Moulton>I mean, that's pretty standard practice, I think. I don't know if that's exactly what's happening right now, or it's just self-organizing groups, or even Twitter bots. I mean, I don't really know. We haven't spent a lot of time digging into it.
<v Seth Moulton>我的意思是，我认为这是相当标准的操作。我不知道现在到底发生了什么，或者只是自发组织的团体，甚至是Twitter机器人。我的意思是，我真的不知道。我们没有花太多时间去深究它。

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:37.490
<v Ira Glass>For a lot of us reading news reports during the three weeks before President Biden dropped out, as the days rolled on and President Biden kept insisting that he didn't think he lost any ground in the race and he wasn't going to listen to anybody but himself on this, the chances of him actually leaving the race seemed to get more and more remote.
<v Ira Glass>对于我们许多在拜登总统退选前三周关注新闻报道的人来说，随着日子的推移，拜登总统一直坚持认为他没有失去选情，他不会听从除他自己以外的任何人的意见，他确实离开竞选的可能性似乎变得越来越遥远。

00:33:37.490 --> 00:33:48.380
<v Ira Glass>But this past Monday, Congressman Moulton told me that from where he sat, the whole thing was still very much an open question, even after the assassination attempt on Donald Trump and that photo with his fist in the air.
<v Ira Glass>但上周一，莫尔顿议员告诉我，从他的角度来看，即使在唐纳德·特朗普遇刺未遂以及那张挥拳照片出来后，整件事仍然是一个悬而未决的问题。

00:33:48.380 --> 00:34:06.080
<v Seth Moulton>I mean, look, from my narrow perspective, and the phone calls I've received, it's intensified calls for President Biden to step aside. It's going to be even harder for Democrats to win after that attempted assassination. And so how do we increase pressure on the White House?
<v Seth Moulton>我的意思是，看，从我狭隘的角度，以及我收到的电话来看，这加强了要求拜登总统退选的呼声。在暗杀企图之后，民主党获胜变得更加困难。那么我们如何增加对白宫的压力？

00:34:06.080 --> 00:34:12.360
<v Ira Glass>I understand that you were on a phone call that President Biden was on this weekend. What can you say about that?
<v Ira Glass>我理解你这个周末参加了一个拜登总统也在其中的电话会议。你能说说关于那个电话会议的情况吗？

00:34:12.360 --> 00:34:45.640
<v Seth Moulton>It was a Zoom meeting. And he was speaking with the new Democratic coalition. And well, there were a few dozen of us on with him. And I'm not going to share details of a confidential meeting. But I'll just say that it was discouraging. It left me feeling affirmed of my position and felt like things are bad and getting worse. I mean, this was not a high-stakes debate. This was just a low-stakes conversation with a bunch of fellow Democrats. But it didn't go terribly well for the president.
<v Seth Moulton>那是一个Zoom会议。他正在与新的民主党联盟通话。嗯，有几十个人和他一起在线。我不会分享机密会议的细节。但我只想说那是令人沮丧的。它让我感到自己的立场被证实了，感觉情况很糟并且正在恶化。我的意思是，这不是一场高风险的辩论。这只是一次低风险的对话，和一群民主党同僚在一起。但对总统来说谈得并不好。

00:34:45.640 --> 00:34:50.920
<v Ira Glass>How so?
<v Ira Glass>怎么说？

00:34:50.920 --> 00:35:16.730
<v Seth Moulton>It just seemed like he's not taking our concerns seriously, making a lot of excuses, on the one hand, citing polls that say he's doing better while dismissing all the bad polls by saying polls don't matter, and just continuing to refuse to answer this question about, how are you going to turn things around, sir?
<v Seth Moulton>看起来他没有认真对待我们的担忧，找了很多借口。一方面引用民调说他做得更好，同时通过说民调不重要来驳回所有糟糕的民调，并继续拒绝回答这个问题：先生，你打算如何扭转局面？

00:35:16.730 --> 00:35:35.260
<v Ira Glass>So as we record this, it's Monday, July 15. 19 members of Congress and one senator are with you, basically, in total, calling for President Biden to step down. And then the rest of the Democratic Party elected officials are on the other side. How is that feeling?
