00:35:35.260 --> 00:35:50.870
<v Seth Moulton>Well, first of all, that's just not an accurate characterization. 19 plus 1 are public. The majority are with me. And so therefore, how does it feel? It feels frustrating.
<v Seth Moulton>嗯，首先，那不是一个准确的定性。19加1是公开的。大多数人是站在我这边的。所以因此，感觉如何？感觉很沮丧。

00:35:50.870 --> 00:36:02.970
<v Ira Glass>Can I ask you, are you in a sort of a perverse situation, where, when you watch the president now on TV, you're sort of hoping for him to slip up? Like, if he slips up more, it'll speed--
<v Ira Glass>我可以问你吗，你是否处在一种反常的境地，当你在电视上看总统时，你是在希望他滑倒？就像，如果他滑倒更多，它会加速——

00:36:02.970 --> 00:36:03.420
<v Seth Moulton>No. No, no no.
<v Seth Moulton>不。不，不，不。

00:36:03.420 --> 00:36:06.710
<v Ira Glass>--him on the path towards having to step down?
<v Ira Glass>——他不得不退选的道路？

00:36:06.710 --> 00:36:22.880
<v Seth Moulton>It's an interesting question. No, I'm not hoping the president will slip up. But I am hoping that something happens to push him over the edge, just to make him realize, to face reality here.
<v Seth Moulton>这是个有趣的问题。不，我不是希望总统滑倒。但我确实希望发生些什么能把他推向边缘，只是让他意识到，去面对现实。

00:36:22.880 --> 00:36:36.860
<v Seth Moulton>I think it's important to say, Ira, that I genuinely love Joe Biden. He's been a great mentor to me. He's consistently given me great advice. I have nothing but good things to say about Joe Biden.
<v Seth Moulton>我认为很重要的一点是，艾拉，我真诚地爱乔·拜登。他一直是我伟大的导师。他一贯给我很好的建议。关于乔·拜登，我只有好话要说。

00:36:36.860 --> 00:36:45.890
<v Ira Glass>Is there some moment between you that especially stands out or you think captures what it's like being-- or what it was like in the past between you and him?
<v Ira Glass>在你们之间有什么时刻特别突出，或者你认为捕捉到了作为——或者过去你和他之间是什么样的？

00:36:45.890 --> 00:37:06.470
<v Seth Moulton>President Biden used to have me over for breakfast when he was vice president. We'd sit at his residence. And of course, breakfast was always scheduled for about an hour and it would go for close to three. And he would regale me with stories from his time in the Senate and the lessons that he's learned about politics over the years.
<v Seth Moulton>拜登总统当副总统时常请我吃早餐。我们会坐在他的官邸里。当然，早餐通常安排大约一个小时，但会持续近三个小时。他会跟我讲述他参议院任职期间的故事，以及他多年来从政治中学到的教训。

00:37:06.470 --> 00:37:31.420
<v Seth Moulton>I'd ask very serious questions, pose situations where I needed advice. And he genuinely took me under his wing. It felt like he was invested in me personally. Now, there are hundreds of people in politics who know Joe Biden better than I. I'm still relatively new to this whole thing. But I like him a lot. I could tell that he cared.
<v Seth Moulton>我会问非常严肃的问题，提出我需要建议的情况。他真诚地把我带在身边。感觉他个人对我有投入。现在，政界有数百人比我更了解乔·拜登。我对这一切还比较陌生。但我很喜欢他。我看得出他在乎。

00:37:31.420 --> 00:37:39.940
<v Ira Glass>Knowing him to the degree that you do, are you surprised that he won't give up?
<v Ira Glass>了解他到你这种程度，你惊讶他不会放弃吗？

00:37:39.940 --> 00:37:58.710
<v Seth Moulton>He's incredibly tenacious. But I also know he's dedicated to our country. And there's a well-worn phrase in the Marines that they try to drill into you day after day in training, which is, it's not about you.
<v Seth Moulton>他极其顽强。但我同时也知道他致力于我们的国家。在海军陆战队中有一句老话，他们试图在训练中一天又一天地灌输给你，那就是：这不是关于你自己的。

00:37:58.710 --> 00:38:31.170
<v Seth Moulton>And that's my fundamental message to President Biden today, is, sir, this is not about you. You're an amazing president. You've been an incredible senator. You've done so much for the United States of America. And now, the best thing you can do is to step aside and give us just a little bit better chance at defeating Donald Trump and winning this election.
<v Seth Moulton>这就是我今天给拜登总统的基本信息，先生，这不是关于你自己的。你是一位了不起的总统。你曾是一位不可思议的参议员。你为美利坚合众国做了这么多。现在，你能做的最好的事情就是退选，给我们一点点更好的机会去击败唐纳德·特朗普，去赢得这场选举。

00:38:31.170 --> 00:38:43.960
<v Ira Glass>Congressman Seth Moulton from Massachusetts. Again, we spoke on Monday, well before the president decided to step down.
<v Ira Glass>来自马萨诸塞州的塞斯·莫尔顿议员。同样，我们在周一进行了交谈，远在总统决定退选之前。

00:38:43.960 --> 00:38:59.140
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, typing with your pinkies, how it has the power to make you rethink everything about your own sister. We have a true-life example. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，用小指打字，它如何有力量让你重新思考关于你妹妹的一切。我们有一个真实生活的例子。那将在芝加哥公共广播电台的节目继续之后的一分钟内。

00:38:59.140 --> 00:39:11.860
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, The Big Rethink, stories of people reconsidering something fundamental about themselves and the way they see themselves and what happens next. We've arrived at Act Three of our show.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目《大重思》，讲述人们重新考虑关于自己以及他们看待自己的方式的一些根本性东西，以及接下来发生了什么的故事。我们已经到达了节目的第三幕。

00:39:11.860 --> 00:39:29.480
<v Ira Glass>Act three, Anything You Can Do, I Can Do Backwards. OK, so in this time of country-defining decisions, we close our show out today with yet another story of critical import about two sisters and their pinkies, a story Elna Baker stumbled on.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，你做到的任何事，我都倒着做。好的，在这个国家决定性的时刻，我们今天用另一个关于两个姐妹和她们的小指的故事结束我们的节目，这是一个具有重要意义的故事，艾尔娜·贝克（Elna Baker）偶然发现的。

00:39:29.480 --> 00:39:52.070
<v Elna Baker>I was chatting with a friend of a friend, Zoe Kushlefsky, at a bar when she casually mentioned she was about to attempt to break the world record for speed typing the alphabet using only her pinkies. It took me a minute to process. There's a world record for typing with your pinkies? How do you even know that's a thing? Where does that idea even come from? And that's when Zoe said--
<v Elna Baker>我在酒吧和朋友的朋友佐伊·库什列夫斯基（Zoe Kushlefsky）聊天时，她随口提到她正要尝试打破世界纪录，用小指打字最快完成字母表。我花了一分钟来处理这个信息。用小指打字还有世界纪录？你怎么知道这是一回事？那个想法是从哪儿来的？那就是佐伊说——

00:39:52.070 --> 00:39:56.240
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>My sister. I stole it.
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>我妹妹。我偷来的。

00:39:56.240 --> 00:40:21.170
<v Elna Baker>Sisters-- so twisted, right? My sister has something, and I want to take it from her. Here's how it all started. One day, Zoe's sister, Eliza, texted her this. "LOL, I don't know if you heard, but I broke a world record tonight, fastest time typing the alphabet backwards with my pinky fingers. LOL, I did it in 6.31 seconds. And the record is 9.65.
<v Elna Baker>姐妹——太纠结了，对吧？我妹妹有一样东西，我想把它从她那夺走。这是这一切的开始。一天，佐伊的妹妹伊莱扎（Eliza）给发了条短信：“哈哈，不知道你听说了没，我今晚打破了一个世界纪录，用小指倒着打字母表的最快时间。哈哈，我用了6.31秒。纪录是9.65。”

00:40:21.170 --> 00:40:33.700
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>My next texts were, "LOL, what? That's so bizarre and cool. Bet I can do it in 6:30. I'm going to ruin your night."
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>我接下来的短信是：“哈哈，什么？那太离奇也太酷了。打赌我能在6.30秒内完成。我要毁了你的夜晚。”

00:40:33.700 --> 00:40:42.880
<v Elna Baker>Wow. So it was sort of instantaneous, your desire to take this record from her?
<v Elna Baker>哇。所以你想从她那儿夺走纪录的愿望是瞬间产生的？

00:40:42.880 --> 00:41:00.520
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>Yeah, I guess so. And I was joking, but it was coming from a true place of, I really do bet I could do it. Like, should I? No, I shouldn't. Unless--
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>是的，我想是的。我当时在开玩笑，但这源于一个真实的想法，我真的敢打赌我能做到。比如，我该做吗？不，我不该做。除非——

00:41:00.520 --> 00:41:11.420
<v Elna Baker>So Zoe opens her laptop and gives it a try, starts typing the alphabet backwards with her pinkies. She's not great, but she's improving rapidly and live-texting Eliza as she gets better.
<v Elna Baker>于是佐伊打开笔记本电脑试了试，开始用小指倒着打字母表。她不太擅长，但她在迅速提高，并在变得更好时实时给伊莱扎发短信。

00:41:11.420 --> 00:41:31.750
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>And she said, "If you keep practicing, I bet you can get it." And I said, "I think I could, too, but I would feel like an asshole, LOL." She said, "They have the same one for alphabet forwards. The record is 5.7 seconds." And I said, "With pinkies? That would be kind of sick if I got forwards and you got backwards, LOL." Got to throw the LOL in there to soften it, as always.
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>她说：“如果你一直练习，我打赌你能做到的。”我说：“我也觉得我能，但我会觉得自己像个混蛋，哈哈。”她说：“他们正向打字母表也有一样的纪录。纪录是5.7秒。”我说：“用小指？如果我能做到正向而你做到倒向，那会很酷的，哈哈。”总是得加个“哈哈”来软化语气。

00:41:31.750 --> 00:41:42.170
<v Elna Baker>Zoe, right then and there, decided that she would go for trying to beat the record for typing the alphabet with your pinkies forward, like she's not dethroning her sister and taking her record.
<v Elna Baker>佐伊就在那一刻，决定去尝试打破用小指正向打字母表的纪录，就像她不是在废黜她妹妹并夺走她的纪录一样。

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:52.130
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>Because to me, that was a way to just ever so slightly sidestep the idea of coming for her gig.
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>因为对我来说，那是一种稍微侧面回避想要抢她风头的想法的方式。

00:41:52.130 --> 00:42:01.190
<v Elna Baker>And did you think that was going to work? Did you think that was believable?
<v Elna Baker>你以为这会管用吗？你以为这可信吗？

00:42:01.190 --> 00:42:06.140
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>No.
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>不。

00:42:06.140 --> 00:42:36.400
<v Elna Baker>There's a specific kind of mind game endemic to sisterhood. And I want to use this stupid pinky thing to examine that, how something so small can blow up because of deep stuff from the past. Like in Zoe's case, from her perspective, Eliza has always been upstaging her. They're both just a few years out of college, but Eliza is already a software engineer with a high-paying job. Zoe's a nanny. Eliza travels the world. Zoe binge watches Vanderpump Rules.
<v Elna Baker>姐妹关系中存在一种特定类型的心理游戏。我想用这件愚蠢的小指琐事来检查这一点，一件如此微小的事情如何因为过去深层的东西而爆发。比如在佐伊的情况下，从她的角度看，伊莱扎一直在抢她的风头。她们都大学毕业几年了，但伊莱扎已经是拥有高薪工作的软件工程师。佐伊是个保姆。伊莱扎环游世界。佐伊在疯狂观看《Vanderpump Rules》。

00:42:36.400 --> 00:42:56.020
<v Elna Baker>And Eliza's always racking up some new achievement. Like, she only came up with the idea to break the world record because she needed something to do after running her first half marathon, whereas Zoe feels left behind in life. She moved to LA to become a TV writer but hasn't had the confidence to try yet.
<v Elna Baker>而伊莱扎总是在积累新的成就。比如，她打破世界纪录的想法只是因为她在跑完第一个半程马拉松后需要找点事做，而佐伊觉得在生活中被落在了后面。她搬到洛杉矶想成为电视编剧，但一直没信心去尝试。

00:42:56.020 --> 00:43:09.860
<v Elna Baker>And this leads me to the second thing you need to know. The day Zoe got the text from Eliza, it was a big day for Zoe. Just that afternoon, she had finished four months of treatment in a super intense eating disorder program.
<v Elna Baker>这就引出了你需要知道的第二件事。佐伊收到伊莱扎短信的那天，对佐伊来说是个大日子。那天下午，她刚刚完成了在一个超级紧张的饮食失调项目中四个月的治疗。

00:43:09.860 --> 00:43:24.040
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>Like, I'm talking eating all my meals in the company of a therapist, basically, or a nutritionist. And now, I was like, OK, now it's time to work myself back into the real world.
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>我的意思是，我在治疗师或营养师的陪伴下吃掉所有的饭菜，基本上。现在，我想，好吧，现在是时候把自己放回真实的世界了。

00:43:24.040 --> 00:43:32.960
<v Elna Baker>Does it feel like she's rubbing her life in your face?
<v Elna Baker>感觉她在把她的生活往你脸上抹吗？

00:43:32.960 --> 00:43:48.810
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>Yes. I mean, for a few good minutes, I was-- I was just kind of like, this was my-- like, I felt really proud of myself for getting through treatment. I felt like I had just kind of had my own accomplishment.
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>是的。我的意思是，有几分钟，我只是觉得——我对完成治疗感到非常自豪。我觉得自己刚完成了一个属于我自己的成就。

00:43:48.810 --> 00:43:59.540
<v Elna Baker>And that's when-- record scratch-- she got the text from Eliza, and photos. Eliza was surrounded by friends. Everyone's raising their pinkies and celebrating.
<v Elna Baker>就在那时——唱片刮擦声——她收到了伊莱扎的短信和照片。伊莱扎被朋友们簇拥着。每个人都在翘着小指庆祝。

00:43:59.540 --> 00:44:30.380
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>And I'm sitting alone on my couch. No one is around me. I'm just sitting, kind of stewing in my own emotions. So I was a little bit-- yeah, I was a little bitter. I was like, doesn't she know it's my last day of treatment? Like, doesn't she know how horrible the timing of this is? I just was bitter. I was just like, congratulate me. This isn't-- this is my moment. This was supposed to be my thing.
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>而我独自坐在沙发上。我身边没人。我只是坐在那儿，沉浸在自己的情绪中。所以我有点——是的，我有点苦涩。我想，难道她不知道这是我治疗的最后一天吗？我的意思是，难道她不知道这个时机有多糟糕吗？我只是苦涩。我只是想，祝贺我。这不是——这是我的时刻。这本该是我的事。

00:44:30.380 --> 00:45:03.610
<v Elna Baker>So Zoe finds a typing speed website that can time her and starts rage-typing with her pinkies. To give you an idea of how absurd this looks, hold up your pinkies like Dr. Evil and try to type anything. Now try to do it faster. Now faster. Now repeat that for months. Insane, right? But Zoe became completely fixated on winning.
<v Elna Baker>于是佐伊找到了一个可以计时打字速度的网站，开始用小指愤怒地打字。为了让你知道这看起来有多荒谬，像邪恶博士那样翘起你的小指，试着打字任何东西。现在试着打得更快。再快。现在重复几个月。疯了，对吧？但佐伊变得完全执着于获胜。

00:45:03.610 --> 00:45:24.790
<v Elna Baker>Zoe and Eliza are stepsisters. When Zoe was eight, her dad met Eliza's mom. And within a couple years, he lived with Eliza's mom and Eliza. Zoe would visit every other weekend. Zoe was already self-conscious about her weight. Her dad was always on her about it. And right away, she started comparing her body to Eliza's.
<v Elna Baker>佐伊和伊莱扎是继姐妹。佐伊八岁时，她爸爸遇见了伊莱扎的妈妈。几年之内，他住进了伊莱扎妈妈和伊莱扎的家。佐伊每隔一个周末来访。佐伊一直对自己的体重很自觉。她爸爸总是唠叨她。很快，她就开始把自己的身体和伊莱扎进行比较。

00:45:24.790 --> 00:45:39.060
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>Eliza, it always just seems to come naturally to. She was always athletic. She ran track. I felt like Eliza was the daughter that my dad dreamed of.
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>伊莱扎，这似乎总是自然而然。她一直很有运动细胞。她跑田径。我觉得伊莱扎是我爸爸梦想中的女儿。

00:45:39.060 --> 00:45:56.190
<v Elna Baker>For dessert, he might serve Zoe fruit, while Eliza got pie. There'd be little critical comments. Zoe's dad had been fat, lost weight his senior year of high school, and felt like it made life so much easier. He thought he was helping Zoe, but now sees how wrong-headed all that was.
<v Elna Baker>吃甜点时，他可能会给佐伊水果，而伊莱扎吃馅饼。会有一些小小的批评意见。佐伊的爸爸曾经很胖，高中最后一年减了肥，觉得这让生活容易多了。他以为他在帮佐伊，但现在看到了这种做法是多么错误。

00:45:56.190 --> 00:46:12.630
<v Elna Baker>But as a teenager, Zoe decided that since Eliza was thin and got good grades and everything seemed easy for her, that Eliza was the favorite, and she was more of a burden. All these past wounds ready to rip open with one ill-timed text.
<v Elna Baker>但作为青少年，佐伊决定，既然伊莱扎很瘦，成绩又好，一切对她来说似乎都很容易，那么伊莱扎就是最受宠的，而她更像是个负担。所有这些过去的伤口都准备好被一条不合时宜的短信撕开。

00:46:12.630 --> 00:46:29.400
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>All this from a pinky world record. It is all connected, but it is pretty wild that all this stuff is coming up.
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>这一切源于一个小指世界纪录。它们都有关联，但这一切涌现出来确实有点狂野。

00:46:29.400 --> 00:46:46.200
<v Elna Baker>Six months go by, and Zoe's getting faster by the millisecond. Early on, she double-checks with Eliza. Are you sure you're OK with this? And Eliza's like, yeah, sure. So Zoe trains on. Finally, she's ready to go for it.
<v Elna Baker>六个月过去了，佐伊打字速度每毫秒都在提高。早些时候，她和伊莱扎再三确认。你确定没问题吗？伊莱扎说，是啊，当然。于是佐伊继续训练。终于，她准备好去挑战了。

00:46:46.200 --> 00:46:55.900
<v Elna Baker>To officially break the record, Zoe has to video her attempt to break the record and send it into Guinness. And so on the day of her first attempt, she invited friends over.
<v Elna Baker>为了正式打破纪录，佐伊必须录下她尝试打破纪录的视频，并将其发给吉尼斯。所以在她第一次尝试的那天，她邀请了朋友过来。

00:46:55.900 --> 00:47:05.110
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>I wore sweatbands around my head and my wrists because it was, like, my athletic, Olympian event.
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>我头上和手腕上都戴着汗带，因为这就像我的运动奥林匹克赛事。

00:47:05.110 --> 00:47:07.020
<v Elna Baker>Like, your Rocky moment.
<v Elna Baker>就像你的《洛奇》时刻？

00:47:07.020 --> 00:47:19.600
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>Yeah, it was fully my Rocky moment. And I should have gotten tiny little sweatbands for my pinkies. That was a missed opportunity. I did my little slate, where I say, my name is Zoe Kushlefsky.
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>是的，完全是我的《洛奇》时刻。我本该给我的小指准备小小的汗带。那是个错失的机会。我做了我的小板子，上面写着，我的名字是佐伊·库什列夫斯基。

00:47:19.600 --> 00:47:20.950
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>Hi, my name is Zoe Kushlefsky.
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>嗨，我的名字是佐伊·库什列夫斯基。

