<v Eliza>Yeah.
<v Eliza>是啊。

00:48:36.550 --> 00:48:39.540
<v Elna Baker>Eliza congratulates her, but also says--
<v Elna Baker>伊莱扎祝贺她，但也说——

00:48:39.540 --> 00:48:52.730
<v Eliza>For me, I'm really over the whole record thing, to be honest. For me, it was-- like, it's almost been-- what-- like, nine months now since I did it? And so I'm very over it. I'm just excited for you. I don't really care anymore, from my perspective. I did what I set out to do.
<v Eliza>对我来说，老实说，我真的对那个纪录的事情感到腻了。对我来说，它——就像——离我做那件事已经过了九个月了？所以我很腻了。我只是为你感到兴奋。我真的不在乎了，从我的角度看。我完成了我打算做的事情。

00:48:52.730 --> 00:49:12.760
<v Eliza>So for me, it was just, yeah, something fun to do. And so it doesn't really matter to me anymore, all the specifics. I don't know. It just depends what it meant to you. I think it could have been-- it was different for you. But for me, I mean, it wore off a while ago. So it passed pretty quickly for me. But for you, it might be different.
<v Eliza>所以对我来说，这只是，是的，做些有趣的事情。所以我不再关心所有细节了。我不知道。这只取决于它对你意味着什么。我认为它可能是——对你来说是不同的。但对我来说，我的意思是，它早就不存在了。所以对我来说过得很快。但对你来说，可能会有所不同。

00:49:12.760 --> 00:49:14.740
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>True, true.
<v Zoe Kushlefsky>真的，真的。

00:49:14.740 --> 00:49:32.720
<v Elna Baker>I felt like I could hear Zoe deflating on the call. She went from cheering and laughing to being ashamed for even caring. This was such a sister counterattack. She seemed so harsh. But when I sat down with Eliza to talk about it, she was warm, self-deprecating.
<v Elna Baker>我觉得我能听到佐伊在电话那头泄气。她从欢呼和欢笑变成了因为自己在意而感到羞耻。这简直是姐妹间的反击。她看起来如此严厉。但当我坐下来和伊莱扎谈论它时，她是热情的、自嘲的。

00:49:32.720 --> 00:49:37.670
<v Eliza>I come in frazzled. My hands have a little ketchup on them.
<v Eliza>我走进来时心烦意乱。我的手上有一点番茄酱。

00:49:37.670 --> 00:49:42.350
<v Elna Baker>So I played the call for her and just asked her, what was up with that?
<v Elna Baker>所以我给她放了录音，只是问她，怎么回事？

00:49:42.350 --> 00:49:44.340
<v Elna Baker>How do you think you sound in this?
<v Elna Baker>你觉得你在这录音里听起来怎么样？

00:49:44.340 --> 00:49:47.870
<v Eliza>Not great. I mean, I think I sound defensive and upset.
<v Eliza>不怎么好。我的意思是，我觉得我听起来很防御且难过。

00:49:47.870 --> 00:49:48.990
<v Elna Baker>Defensive?
<v Elna Baker>防御？

00:49:48.990 --> 00:50:03.870
<v Eliza>I think so. I'm like, I don't even really care. But for you, it's different. Oh, this matters to you, but it doesn't matter to me. For you, it's good.
<v Eliza>我想是的。我说，我根本不在乎。但对你来说，这不同。哦，这对你很重要，但对我来说不重要。对你来说，这很好。

00:50:03.870 --> 00:50:36.750
<v Elna Baker>Here's the thing. Eliza knew Zoe was recording her. And in good sister fashion, she'd said she was OK with it, but she wasn't really. So she was flustered and uncomfortable. And she had other feelings, too. This itch Zoe has to take her down, Eliza's noticed, and it doesn't feel great. If you look at the story from her perspective, Eliza breaks a world record. And when she first texts her sister, hey, I did this cool thing. Zoe's response is, fuck your thing.
<v Elna Baker>事情是这样的。伊莱扎知道佐伊在录音。按照姐妹的方式，她说没关系，但她其实并不这么想。所以她惊慌失措且不舒服。她也有其他感受。佐伊想打倒她的那种痒，伊莱扎注意到了，而且感觉不太好。如果你从她的角度看这个故事，伊莱扎打破了世界纪录。当她第一次发短信给妹妹，嘿，我做了这件很酷的事。佐伊的回应是，去你的事。

00:50:36.750 --> 00:50:56.780
<v Eliza>And I guess I was just like, why is her first response to compete with me and one-up me? And I was just like, instead of being happy for me, she saw it as this competition. And her first response was, I can do it better. This is this cool thing that I tried to achieve. And now you're trying to beat it.
<v Eliza>我想我当时的想法是，为什么她第一次回应是跟我竞争并试图超过我？我只是觉得，她没为我高兴，却把它看作是一种竞争。她的第一次回应是，我能做得更好。这是我努力实现的很酷的事。现在你却试图打败它。

00:50:56.780 --> 00:50:58.960
<v Elna Baker>Yeah, why are you coming after me?
<v Elna Baker>是的，你为什么要来找我麻烦？

00:50:58.960 --> 00:51:05.410
<v Eliza>Yeah. And I think it's the fact that her first text after was, I bet I can do it faster.
<v Eliza>是的。我想这就是事实，因为之后的第一条短信是，我打赌我能打得更快。

00:51:05.410 --> 00:51:08.030
<v Elna Baker>Right, she didn't even say congratulations first.
<v Elna Baker>对，她甚至没先说恭喜。

00:51:08.030 --> 00:51:17.770
<v Eliza>Yeah. So then it's like, if that's where this is coming from, that feels hurtful. The entire way it was phrased was just doing it better than me.
<v Eliza>是啊。所以这就感觉很伤人。整个表达方式只是为了比我做得更好。

00:51:17.770 --> 00:51:32.960
<v Elna Baker>Eliza didn't say any of this to Zoe, though. She felt like it was a dumb thing to get her feelings hurt over. So instead, she texted Zoe, I dare you. Eliza says, Zoe always trying to one-up her this way-- Eliza doesn't get it.
<v Elna Baker>伊莱扎并没有对佐伊说这些。她觉得因为这点事受伤很傻。所以她发短信给佐伊，我敢打赌你做不到。伊莱扎说，佐伊总是试图通过这种方式超过她——伊莱扎不明白。

00:51:32.960 --> 00:51:37.930
<v Elna Baker>You personally have never felt like you've been competing with Zoe?
<v Elna Baker>你个人从来没觉得你在跟佐伊竞争吗？

00:51:37.930 --> 00:51:55.310
<v Eliza>Not competing. To me, it's not even comparable. I've always admired the fact that she's out in LA, living her dreams. And it's just that she is in a very different career path. But I always think everything she is doing there is amazing. And she's married. And she has a house.
<v Eliza>不是竞争。对我来说，这甚至没有可比性。我一直钦佩她身在洛杉矶，过着她的梦想。只是她是在一个非常不同的职业道路上。但我一直觉得她在那里做的每一件事都很棒。而且她结婚了。她有一栋房子。

00:51:55.310 --> 00:52:17.580
<v Elna Baker>Yes, Zoe is married. She lives in a nice house. She and her wife have a great relationship. Eliza hasn't yet checked off those big life boxes. She's also, Eliza told me, the performer of the family, the one who upstages her. Plus, Zoe's the oldest. She's actually the big sister. Eliza's like, you win.
<v Elna Baker>是的，佐伊结婚了。她住在一栋漂亮的房子里。她和她妻子关系很好。伊莱扎还没有勾选那些大的人生选项。她也是，伊莱扎告诉我，家里那个表演者，那个抢她风头的人。加上，佐伊是最大的。她实际上是大姐姐。伊莱扎说，你赢了。

00:52:17.580 --> 00:52:40.710
<v Eliza>I felt like she set the tone. Like, she was player one on the Wii. She had to go to the bathroom first when we got home from the car ride. Like, not in any resentful way, just in like a, that was the pecking order. You're the oldest sibling. Like, that felt right. And I think she even said to me, too. I think she said she feels like there's a point where it flipped. And to her, I felt more like the older sister.
<v Eliza>我觉得她定下了基调。就像，她是Wii上的第一玩家。当我们在车里回到家时，她得先去洗手间。不是那种怨恨的方式，只是那种，那才是等级顺序。你是最大的兄弟姐妹。我觉得那是对的。而且我想她甚至也对我说了。我想她说她觉得有一个点翻转了。对她来说，我觉得更像大姐姐。

00:52:40.710 --> 00:53:02.490
<v Eliza>But that never happened for me. I've always seen her as the older sister. I still look up to her a lot. I think I still really like to get her approval. I don't know if she said. But actually, someone made a comment about that recently in our family, that I like to get her approval. So to me, I've always looked up to her and admired her, and I still do.
<v Eliza>但那对我来说从未发生。我一直视她为大姐姐。我仍然非常仰望她。我想我仍然真的很想得到她的认可。我不知道她是否说过。但实际上，最近家里有人对此发表了评论，说我喜欢得到她的认可。所以对我来说，我一直仰望她并钦佩她，而且我仍然这么做。

00:53:02.490 --> 00:53:16.990
<v Elna Baker>She told me she had no idea that the day she texted Zoe about her world record was the day Zoe ended treatment. And Zoe never said anything. And she also had no idea that from the time they were kids, Zoe felt inferior to her.
<v Elna Baker>她告诉我，她完全不知道她发短信给佐伊关于世界纪录那天是佐伊结束治疗的那天。佐伊从未说什么。她也完全不知道从她们小时候起，佐伊就觉得不如她。

00:53:16.990 --> 00:53:18.610
<v Eliza>Yeah, that's shocking to me.
<v Eliza>是的，那对我来说太震惊了。

00:53:18.610 --> 00:53:19.580
<v Elna Baker>Shocking?
<v Elna Baker>震惊？

00:53:19.580 --> 00:53:30.690
<v Eliza>Yeah. I mean, I just don't see myself in that role. Like, wow. Like, she's doing so much better. I didn't realize she felt like that.
<v Eliza>是啊。我的意思是，我并不把自己看作那个角色。哇。我的意思是，她做得好多了。我没意识到她会有那种感觉。

00:53:30.690 --> 00:53:41.040
<v Elna Baker>Eliza says that growing up, she had no idea Zoe thought Eliza was the favorite, the daughter their dad really wanted. She never noticed that she got dessert and Zoe got fruit.
<v Elna Baker>伊莱扎说，成长过程中，她完全不知道佐伊认为伊莱扎是最受宠的，是他们爸爸真正想要的女儿。她从没注意到她得到了甜点，而佐伊得到了水果。

00:53:41.040 --> 00:54:00.720
<v Eliza>I mean, it makes me sad to think that. Some of that, I think, especially now that we're older, I'm very aware of. But I definitely, again, wasn't as a child. And it makes me sad to think of Zoe going through all that and feeling very alone and me having no idea. Like, that is so isolating.
<v Eliza>我的意思是，想到那让我感到难过。我想这其中一些，特别是在我们长大后，我是非常清楚的。但我肯定，再次重申，作为孩子时我并不知道。想到佐伊经历这一切并感到非常孤独，而我一无所知，这让我感到难过。我的意思是，那太孤立了。

00:54:00.720 --> 00:54:06.340
<v Elna Baker>But then I also see it from your perspective, because it sort of has nothing to do with you.
<v Elna Baker>但然后我也从你的角度看它，因为它与你没有关系。

00:54:06.340 --> 00:54:36.110
<v Eliza>But it does-- like, I don't see it from my perspective. It does have to do with me, I mean, even if it doesn't directly. Like, I wish I could have been there for her and-- yeah, I don't know why I wasn't. Like, I don't know, just being younger and just not seeing these things. And maybe it's not something that I was primed to think about. So I wasn't even noticing.
<v Eliza>但它确实有关系——我的意思是，我不是从我的角度看它。它与我有关系，我的意思是，即使不是直接相关。我的意思是，我希望我当时能陪在她身边——是的，我不知道为什么我没做到。我的意思是，只是因为更年轻而且没有看到这些事情。也许这不是我天生会去想的事情。所以我当时根本没注意到。

00:54:36.110 --> 00:54:55.670
<v Elna Baker>Since this whole pinky record thing, for the first time, Zoe and Eliza have started talking about all this, directly. They called each other, compared notes. Zoe was like, oops, I didn't say congratulations after you got your record, did I? And Eliza was like, I had no idea you thought my job was cool.
<v Elna Baker>自从整个小指纪录这件事以来，佐伊和伊莱扎第一次开始直接谈论这一切。她们打电话给对方，比较笔记。佐伊说，哎呀，我没在妳拿到纪录后说恭喜，对吧？伊莱扎说，我根本不知道妳觉得我的工作很酷。

00:54:55.670 --> 00:55:27.100
<v Elna Baker>And Zoe, who in one sentence will say she admires Eliza and in the next say she's jealous, has realized that they're two sides of the same coin, and how she feels about Eliza in any given moment has everything to do with how she feels about herself. And she's being more generous with her. It all looks different now, to both of them.
